-length vector of word counts. It ignores grammar and word order.\n\nMechanism: If our vocabulary is $\\{ \\text{\"dress\"}, \\text{\"fabric\"}, \\text{\"bad\"} \\}$, the sentence \"bad dress\" is represented as $[1, 0, 1]$.\n\nTF-IDF (Term Frequency - Inverse Document Frequency)\n\nTF-IDF improves upon BoW by weighing words based on their importance. It reduces the weight of common words (like \"the\", \"is\") and increases the weight of rare, descriptive words.\n\n2. Modern Approach: Contextual Embeddings (BERT)\n\nUnlike TF-IDF, which generates a static vector for each word (i.e., \"bank\" has the same vector in \"river bank\" and \"bank account\"), BERT generates contextual embeddings.\n\nMechanism: BERT uses a tokenizer to convert words into Input IDs. These IDs are passed through multiple Transformer layers. The resulting vector for each token depends on all other tokens in the sentence.\n\nAdvantage: This allows the model to capture polysemy (words with multiple meanings) and complex syntactic structures, which is crucial for distinguishing between aspect sentiments (e.g., \"The fit is great but the material is cheap\").","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"Step 1: Load Dataset","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"# Load the dataset\ntry:\n    df = pd.read_csv('cleaned_reviews_for_annotation.csv')\n    df = df.dropna(subset=['cleaned_text'])\n    print(f\"Data loaded. Shape: {df.shape}\")\nexcept FileNotFoundError:\n    print(\"File not found. Loading dummy data...\")\n    df = pd.DataFrame({'cleaned_text': ['dress nice', 'dress bad'], 'sentiment': ['Positive', 'Negative']})\n\n\n","metadata":{"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2025-11-25T13:29:56.967604Z","iopub.execute_input":"2025-11-25T13:29:56.967896Z","iopub.status.idle":"2025-11-25T13:29:57.030215Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2025-11-25T13:29:56.967878Z","shell.execute_reply":"2025-11-25T13:29:57.029637Z"}},"outputs":[{"name":"stdout","text":"Data loaded. Shape: 