BERT is based on the Transformer architecture (Vaswani et al., 2017), specifically the Encoder stack. The key innovation utilized here is the Self-Attention Mechanism, which allows the model to weigh the importance of different words in a sentence relative to each other.\n\n\n**Task-Specific Implementation (Fine-Tuning)**\n\nThis project involves two distinct NLP tasks, requiring two different \"Heads\" to be attached to the pre-trained BERT base.\n\n- Task A: Aspect Term Extraction (Sequence Labeling)\n\nObjective: To identify specific words in the review (e.g., \"fabric\", \"zipper\") that represent product aspects.\n\nModel Structure: BERT for Token Classification.\n\nInput: A sequence of tokens $T = \\{t_1, t_2, ..., t_n\\}$.\n\nOutput: A sequence of labels $L = \\{l_1, l_2, ..., l_n\\}$ using the IOB format (Inside, Outside, Beginning).\n\nMechanism: We feed the hidden state of every token into a dense classification layer to predict if that specific token is an aspect.\n\n- Task B: Aspect Sentiment Classification (Sequence Classification)\n\nObjective: To classify the sentiment (Positive, Negative, Neutral) relative to the extracted aspect.\n\nModel Structure: BERT for Sequence Classification.\n\nInput: The [CLS] token (which holds the aggregate representation of the sentence) followed by the sentence tokens and the aspect tokens.\n\nOutput: A softmax probability distribution over the sentiment classes.\n\nMechanism: We utilize the embedding of the special [CLS] token. This embedding is passed through a fully connected layer (Dropout $\\rightarrow$ Linear $\\rightarrow$ Softmax) to output the final sentiment probability.\n\n$$P(y|x) = \\text{softmax}(W \\cdot h$$","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"# **MODEL TRAINING**","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"**Cell 1:** Load Data and Prepare Labels We reload your cleaned CSV and convert \"Positive/Negative\" into numbers (0, 1, 2) so BERT can understand them.","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"# 1. Load the cleaned data from your previous step\ntry:\n    df = pd.read_csv('cleaned_reviews_for_annotation.csv')\n    df = df.dropna(subset=['cleaned_text', 'sentiment']) # Ensure no empty rows\nexcept FileNotFoundError:\n    print(\"Error: 'cleaned_reviews_for_annotation.csv' not found. Please run the Preprocessing steps first.\")\n\n# 2. Map String Labels to Numbers\n# BERT requires integers: Negative=0, Neutral=1, Positive=2\nlabel_map = {'Negative': 0, 'Neutral': 1, 'Positive': 2}\ndf['label'] = df['sentiment'].map(label_map)\n\n# Remove any rows where mapping failed (e.g. if 'Unknown' exists)\ndf = df.dropna(subset=['label'])\ndf['label'] = df['label'].astype(int)\n\n# 3. Split Data: 80% for Training, 20% for Testing\ntrain_df, test_df = train_test_split(df, test_size=0.2, random_state=42)\n\nprint(f\"Training Data Size: {len(train_df)}\")\nprint(f\"Testing Data Size: {len(test_df)}\")","metadata":{"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2025-11-25T13:30:13.416958Z","iopub.execute_input":"2025-11-25T13:30:13.417225Z","iopub.status.idle":"2025-11-25T13:30:13.480670Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2025-11-25T13:30:13.417196Z","shell.execute_reply":"2025-11-25T13:30:13.480026Z"}},"outputs":[{"name":"stdout","text":"Training Data Size: 18107\nTesting Data Size: 4527\n","output_type":"stream"}],"execution_count":18},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"**Cell 2:** Tokenize and Create Datasets We convert the pandas dataframes into Hugging Face Datasets and tokenize them.","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"# Load Tokenizer\ntokenizer = BertTokenizer.from_pretrained('bert-base-uncased')\n\n# Convert pandas dataframe to Hugging Face Dataset\ntrain_dataset = Dataset.from_pandas(train_df)\ntest_dataset = Dataset.from_pandas(test_df)\n\n# Tokenization Function\ndef tokenize_function(examples):\n    return tokenizer(examples[\"cleaned_text\"], padding=\"max_length\", truncation=True, max_length=128)\n\n# Apply Tokenization\ntokenized